{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Browser returns 404</td><td>Does the generated URL match the file-server mount and published filename?</td></tr><tr><td>Bare module name cannot resolve</td><td>Is the import map present before the module script, with the expected alias?</td></tr><tr><td>No expected JavaScript behavior</td><td>Did browser code actually import and initialize the module?</td></tr><tr><td>Missing default export or module syntax error</td><td>Is the selected file ESM, classic JavaScript, or CommonJS?</td></tr><tr><td>Styles are absent</td><td>Was a CSS file selected and its stylesheet link included?</td></tr><tr><td>Runtime still requests a CDN</td><td>Check the shim URL, <code>Raw</code> imports and remote imports inside selected module files.</td></tr><tr><td>A different working directory breaks assets</td><td>Use an explicit RootDir for preparation and an absolute file-server directory.</td></tr><tr><td>Browsers keep an old file</td><td>Do the URLs carry <code>?v=</code>? Use <code>FromFS</code> or <code>WithAssets</code>, and <code>WithVersionedPaths</code> for files scripts load by relative paths.</td></tr><tr><td>The browser refuses a file for its integrity</td><td>Did the file change after the page was rendered? While developing with <code>assets.Reload</code>, render with <code>WithStableIntegrity</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>The policy blocks the import map</td><td>Allow it by hash (<code>ScriptHash</code>, <code>RenderWithHash</code>) with the options the page renders with, or by nonce (<code>WithNonce</code>).</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Vendoring errors</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>… is missing</code></td><td>Is the file in the file system given to <code>FromFS</code>? A <code>go:embed</code> directory pattern leaves out names that start with <code>_</code>; use <code>all:</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>… does not match its integrity; vendor again</code></td><td>A file changed after vendoring. Restore it, or run <code>importmap vendor</code> again.</td></tr><tr><td><code>… is outside the file system</code></td><td>A local package's files are outside the out directory. Load the lock from a file system that holds both, and verify with <code>-root</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>unknown lockfileVersion</code></td><td>The lock has a format this version does not read. Vendor again.</td></tr><tr><td><code>no files match the package's Require patterns</code></td><td>Do the <code>file</code> patterns match the provider's file listing?</td></tr><tr><td><code>not in importmap.lock.json; vendor the packages first</code></td><td><code>vendor -local</code> keeps the other packages' entries, so they must be in the lock. Run <code>importmap vendor</code> first.</td></tr><tr><td><code>unknown field</code></td><td>The configuration has a field the command does not know; check its spelling.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Inspect the prepared outputs</h2>
<pre><code>data, err := im.MarshalIndent()
if err != nil { return err }
fmt.Println(string(data))</code></pre>
<p>Compare the structure with the public asset tree and the HTML head. Provider file paths may differ from the path used in an example for another provider. A matching alias does not make incompatible JavaScript formats interchangeable.</p>
<h2>Cache recovery</h2>
<p>If published files are missing but the selected cache files are complete, <code>CacheOrFetch</code> rebuilds them locally, file by file, and with <code>WithLogger</code> logs <code>asset not found, building from cache</code> for each one. A missing cache file requires network access. Avoid indiscriminate cleaning when the assets directory also holds your own files.</p>
<h2>Concurrent use</h2>
<p>Treat an import map as preparation-time configuration. Fetching mutates its mappings and files; configure and prepare it before sharing it with HTTP handlers. Rendering only reads it, so it is then safe from many goroutines. While developing, a manifest made with <code>assets.Reload</code> follows files edited on disk; leave it out in production.</p>
</article>
